    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S1836\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the election law, in relation to permitting election\ninspectors and poll clerks to work half shifts\n\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nPermits boards of elections to engage election inspectors and poll\nclerks to work half day shifts, their compensation to be prorated\naccordingly.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 adds a new subdivision 7 to section 3-400 of the election\nlaw providing that every board of elections may engage election\ninspectors and poll clerks to work half day shifts, with their\ncompensation to be prorated accordingly.\n\nSection 2 provides that this act shall take effect on the one hundred\ntwentieth day after it shall have become a law.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nMany local boards of elections have a very difficult time recruiting\nand retaining enough election inspectors and poll clerks to adequately\nstaff their polling sites on primary and election days. This problem\nis exacerbated by low wages and the fact that polling places are\ncurrently open for up to fourteen hours, meaning that election workers\nare required to work up to sixteen hours in a single shift. For older\nworkers and workers responsible for the care of young children, these\nhours are simply too burdensome. The local boards could recruit and\nretain more election workers if they offered a more humane work\nschedule. Allowing half day shifts would make election work more\nattractive to the temporary workers the election boards seek to\nrecruit.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n2002 - A6647 - Referred to Elections\n2004 - A3242 - Referred to Elections\n2006 - S4426 - Referred to Elections\n2008 - S2254 - Referred to Elections\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred twentieth day after it\nshall have become a law; provided that the state board of elections is\nauthorized to promulgate any rules necessary to implement this act on\nor before such effective date.\n",
